WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.200
Cisco's 6% drop after a revenue beat shows TFSA holders why order trends matter more than headline EPS. This is Modern Investing Techniques, Episode 231. Friday's close, a simulated state street hold, and a home bias check. Then the trade review, the tools, and the record. Quick reminder, everything we discuss here is for education and entertainment. The practice investment of the day uses simulated trades with no real money. I'm not a licensed financial advisor,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.500
and this isn't financial advice. Always do your own research before putting real

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.160
money to work. United States benchmarks finished mixed on Friday with the S&amp;P 500

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:50.760
at 7,562 down 0.4% and the NASDAQ composite at 25,136 down 0.9%. That NASDAQ

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:57.340
composite print is up 8.18% year to date and up 12.34% since the practice portfolio

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:06.340
opened on March 16th, 2026. The Toronto Stock Exchange Composite, or TSX, finished at 36,106,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:14.100
down only 0.1%. As real estate, utilities, staples, and telcos offset a tape where AI stocks dragged

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.540
harder in the United States. Yesterday, the timing of the next Federal Reserve move was still the

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:25.240
open question. Today, Fed Governor Bowman said the time has come to shift focus to lowering inflation

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:30.540
after recent consumer price index, or CPI, prints showed broadening pressures.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:36.260
The Fed also moved its late December meeting into November, creating longer gaps between

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.920
decisions that could allow larger or unscheduled rate moves starting in 2027.

00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:45.779
Cisco printed fiscal first quarter revenue of $18.5 billion.

00:01:46.459 --> 00:01:53.459
The Globe and Mail reported, up 24% year-over-year, with non-GAP net income of $5.6 billion.

00:01:53.459 --> 00:01:59.399
dollars. Orders for a critical hardware category still declines sequentially, and that gap is the

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:04.839
earnings quality assessment. You look past the headline revenue and earnings per share, or EPS,

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:10.259
beat and test whether the growth drivers are durable. You open the release, you compare the

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:15.399
order trend commentary with prior quarters, and you cross-check it against sector rotation before

00:02:15.399 --> 00:02:20.579
you even think about a position. Picture a restaurant that rang a huge Saturday till while the

00:02:20.579 --> 00:02:22.799
reservation book for next week is half empty.

00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:25.339
Last night's receipts can look like a boom

00:02:25.339 --> 00:02:27.279
while the forward book is already thinning.

00:02:27.779 --> 00:02:29.679
And that is what sequential order softness

00:02:29.679 --> 00:02:31.539
is doing inside a fat revenue print.

00:02:32.119 --> 00:02:35.459
You see revenue up 24% and a key hardware order line

00:02:35.459 --> 00:02:36.859
down from the prior quarter.

00:02:37.479 --> 00:02:39.139
Do you buy the beat or stand aside?

00:02:39.799 --> 00:02:40.919
Here's how I'd think about it.

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:42.619
The decision this technique makes

00:02:42.619 --> 00:02:44.579
is whether the beat is a durable driver

00:02:44.579 --> 00:02:46.159
or a backward looking receipt.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.979
And you do not answer that from EPS alone.

00:02:48.979 --> 00:02:53.959
The method worked in past hardware cycles when early order softness proceeded multiple

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:58.719
quarters of margin pressure, which is why the sequential line gets flagged before the

00:02:58.719 --> 00:02:59.359
multiple does.

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:04.559
It fails when a one-time external event, such as the Nvidia-related sell-off that hit

00:03:04.559 --> 00:03:10.179
the sector Friday, masks otherwise solid fundamentals, so a weak tape can punish a

00:03:10.179 --> 00:03:12.839
clean book and a fat print can hide a weak book.

00:03:13.199 --> 00:03:15.159
You still read the order line either way.

00:03:15.159 --> 00:03:20.239
Pull the transcript or the release in your brokerage research tab, flag the sequential

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:23.579
metric, and set an alert for the next quarter's order commentary.

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:26.719
Remember, the receipt is not the reservation book.

00:03:27.661 --> 00:03:32.901
State Street, ticker STT on the New York Stock Exchange, is the simulated weekly hold

00:03:32.901 --> 00:03:38.241
and Yahoo Finance is where the post earnings drift shows up as a 3.6% gain since the

00:03:38.241 --> 00:03:39.201
last earnings report.

00:03:39.761 --> 00:03:42.041
This is a simulated trade for learning purposes.

00:03:42.041 --> 00:03:51.021
shares only $1,000, entering at Monday's open under the published rules and judged against the NASDAQ composite over its own five sessions.

00:03:51.541 --> 00:03:59.401
The strategy family is valuation. A valuation entry after positive post-earnings drift in a financial name showing relative strength.

00:04:00.001 --> 00:04:06.641
And in one clause it is using earnings quality assessment by waiting for confirmation after the print instead of chasing the headline.

00:04:06.641 --> 00:04:13.501
That valuation approach has delivered plus 1.48% mean alpha across 26 prior trades when

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:17.401
we waited for post earnings confirmation rather than the initial print.

00:04:17.961 --> 00:04:22.481
And the sector concentration rule is why this hold goes into financials instead of stacking

00:04:22.481 --> 00:04:25.881
another technology name after last week's simulated chip hold.

00:04:26.421 --> 00:04:31.381
Technical setup is price action above recent lows with that positive post earnings momentum.

00:04:31.381 --> 00:04:36.081
And the volume filter is whether activity holds up relative to the 20-day average.

00:04:36.521 --> 00:04:42.841
The stop loss sits 8% below entry and caps the loss at $80 on the $1,000 position if

00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:47.701
it is breached intraday, with a target of plus 3% to plus 7%.

00:04:47.701 --> 00:04:52.821
Confidence is medium, earnings momentum and sector rotation alignment support the setup,

00:04:53.301 --> 00:04:56.721
but broader rate path uncertainty remains the unaligned factor.

00:04:56.721 --> 00:05:01.441
If State Street finishes a session under its 50-day moving average on the daily chart

00:05:01.441 --> 00:05:04.561
before those five days are up, the thesis is done.

00:05:05.141 --> 00:05:10.761
That close is the invalidation, named before Monday's cash open, and there is no discretionary

00:05:10.761 --> 00:05:11.621
exit around it.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:18.450
AMD was the last weekly hold, simulated from Monday's open through Friday's close, evaluation

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:23.970
entry after a strong year-to-date run and historical pattern analysis, stated at medium

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:31.530
confidence with an 8% stop below entry. We simulated buying it at $473.39 on the Monday

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:42.670
open and exiting at $534.06 on Friday's close, a gain of 12.82% or $128.16 on the $1,000

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:49.970
position. The NASDAQ composite over those same five sessions rose 1.45%, so the matched

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:57.030
window alpha was plus 11.37%. And honestly, that feels pretty good. The stop was never reached

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:02.190
and the invalidation level was never tested. So the risk control sat unused while the price

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:07.090
did the work. Medium was the right grade for a valuation setup with supportive historical

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:12.430
patterns that then played out cleanly rather than a high confidence pile-in. And the sector

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:17.710
rotation tag on that hold is also why today's simulated ticket is not another technology name.

