00:09:47.157 --> 00:09:54.337
Freehold royalties paying 6.3% inside a 25,000 Canadian dollar TFA still sits in a market

00:09:54.337 --> 00:09:57.357
that is only 3% of global equity capitalization.

00:09:58.217 --> 00:10:03.237
Financials and energy together make up roughly half the TSX, so a long rotation away from

00:10:03.237 --> 00:10:08.477
those two sectors leaves a Canada heavy sleeve lagging global benchmarks even when a single

00:10:08.477 --> 00:10:09.297
holding behaves.

00:10:09.877 --> 00:10:15.177
The monthly 9 cent dividend has been stable since September 2022, and the stock rose

00:10:15.177 --> 00:10:21.417
5.1% to $17.28 on November 11 after strong third quarter results.

00:10:21.417 --> 00:10:26.517
Yet the whole energy royalty group remains tied to commodity cycles that are not the global

00:10:26.517 --> 00:10:27.557
opportunity set.

00:10:28.057 --> 00:10:35.237
Over 25 years of reinvestment at a constant 6.3% yield, that same $25,000 Canadian would

00:10:35.237 --> 00:10:46.437
grow to $118,785.70 with zero price appreciation, throwing off $618.68 a month tax-free, and

00:10:46.437 --> 00:10:49.477
it would still live inside 3% of world capitalization.

00:10:49.477 --> 00:10:56.997
Add the annual average Canada pension plan, or CPP, of $10,300 in 8 cents, and maximum

00:10:56.997 --> 00:11:04.657
old age security, or OAS, of $9,150 at age 65, and you can build a retirement income stack

00:11:04.657 --> 00:11:11.297
without OAS clawback risk, because TFSA withdrawals are not taxable, but the same concentration

00:11:11.297 --> 00:11:16.337
that help the royalty can cap upside when global healthcare or technology outperforms.

00:11:16.337 --> 00:11:23.097
The common misconception is that currency hedging and the dividend tax credit inside a TFA justify

00:11:23.097 --> 00:11:29.357
30-50% home exposure. The fix is to treat your job, your home, and your pension as already

00:11:29.357 --> 00:11:35.257
providing Canada-specific risk and to size the equity sleeve against that. Open the geographic

00:11:35.257 --> 00:11:41.377
allocation report, note the Canada percentage, and compare it with MSCI World or the MSCI

00:11:41.377 --> 00:11:46.917
all-country world index, or ACWI, weights rather than with what feels familiar.

00:11:47.357 --> 00:11:52.017
Then export that breakdown once a quarter and cap Canadian equity at a weight that still

00:11:52.017 --> 00:11:55.297
leaves room for the remaining 97% of global markets.

00:11:55.797 --> 00:12:01.657
This weekend, open the statement, export the TFSA geographic mix, and write down the exact

00:12:01.657 --> 00:12:04.597
Canada percentage versus the MSCI world weight.

